---
/**
 * Home `/` — three editorial sections, each a cover grid (W5 rebuild
 * 2026-09-06, replacing the board-panels + "최신 글" list):
 *
 *   Charts        · 올해의 앨범   — the nominate board. A LIST surface, so it
 *                                   shows and ranks scores (D2).
 *   Latest Reviews · 최신 리뷰     — a BROWSING surface that D2-R2 (2026-09-07)
 *                                   opted back into scores: ArticleItem now
 *                                   carries a `score` field, and this section
 *                                   prints it via ArticleCard's opt-in
 *                                   `showScore` (see lib/derive/lists.ts for
 *                                   the full accounting of what replaced the
 *                                   old type-level guard).
 *   Notes         · 음악 이야기    — browsing, typographic cards (a story has
 *                                   no album art and never will, and no score
 *                                   to carry).
 *
 * WHICH sections appear is decided in the derive layer (`homeSections`), not
 * here; the page renders the result and picks a layout from the count (P1).
 * Score exposure is no longer a single cutoff by section order (D2-R2):
 * Charts and Latest Reviews both print figures, each opted in at its own
 * call site below; Notes stays score-free because a story never has one.
 * build.dist-matrix asserts the surface list in BOTH directions.
 *
 * ALL THREE SECTIONS ALWAYS RENDER (2026-09-07). R-4 ("빈 껍데기 금지") is
 * released FOR THESE THREE SECTIONS ONLY — see SectionEmpty for the scope and
 * for what R-4 still governs elsewhere. A section with no cards keeps its
 * heading and shows an empty, wordless area: no apology, and no sentence
 * describing what the section would contain.
 *
 * CARD SIZE NO LONGER DEPENDS ON CARD COUNT (2026-09-07, final form). The
 * browsing grids are ONE grid — four columns on the desktop, stepping to
 * 3/2/1 as the frame narrows — and the number of cards in a section changes
 * nothing about it. One card fills the first cell and leaves three empty;
 * four fill the row.
 *
 * This reverses two earlier passes, and the reversal is the point worth
 * recording. The grids were first `auto-fill` (the frame decided how many
 * columns), then count-driven (`n-1`…`n-4`, so three cards got three
 * columns) because an empty track on the right was read as a missing card.
 * The editor has since accepted the empty cells, and what that buys is the
 * property those variants cost: A CARD IS THE SAME SIZE ON EVERY PAGE LOAD.
 * Under the count-driven rule a section holding one review rendered it as a
 * 1231px feature and the same review as a 296px card the day a second one
 * was published — the reader's sense of "how big is a review card here" was
 * a function of how much had been published that week.
 *
 * The `n-*` variants and ArticleCard's whole `feature` layout were DELETED
 * rather than left dormant: a branch nobody can reach is a question the next
 * reader has to answer before they can change anything. Charts kept its own
 * count classes for a while after this cut — it was a carousel, a different
 * object with a different job — until the reversal below retired the
 * carousel itself and took the classes with it.
 *
 * The steps and the widths that trigger them are in the grid rules below;
 * the 320px track cap that keeps the hierarchy invariant true at every one
 * of them is unchanged, and deriveHomeSections still caps a section at four
 * — which is now exactly one full row rather than a coincidence.
 *
 * DENSITY IS THE HIERARCHY, AND IT IS A RULE, NOT A SETTING (2026-09-07).
 * Charts is what this site is for; the two browsing sections are not. Both
 * browsing sections take the SAME density on purpose — they are siblings, and
 * giving 음악 이야기 its own third size would invent a middle rank the
 * editorial structure does not have.
 *
 * The invariant, stated so it can be checked: A CHART CARD IS NEVER NARROWER
 * THAN A BROWSING CARD, at any combination of card counts.
 *
 * The first attempt at this failed and is worth recording, because the
 * failure is easy to repeat. "Charts keeps its grid, 최신 리뷰 goes 4-up" put
 * Charts at 5x195px against browsing's 4x248px and INVERTED the hierarchy it
 * was meant to create — the section that matters got the smaller card. Column
 * COUNT is not size. What fixes it is fixing the chart card's width so its
 * cell cannot shrink as the board fills, and capping every browsing track at
 * 320px so a thin section cannot balloon past a full one. Now that the
 * browsing card's width depends only on the viewport, the invariant has one
 * fewer variable in it — the measured table is in
 * 08-impl-notes/frontend.md.
 *
 * The two remaining variant faces are unchanged: `empty` (no reviews at all)
 * prints the two-line declaration, and `post-finalize` puts last year's
 * finalized-list card above the sections. `early` and `normal` now render
 * through the same path — the difference between them was the progress strip
 * ("올해의 앨범 후보가 여기서 자랍니다 — 지금까지 평론 n편"), which the brief
 * cut, and the card-count system covers the thin state better than a banner.
 *
 * The four variants stay distinguishable in the built HTML, which is what
 * the build e2e suite pins:
 *   empty         · the declaration line + three empty grounds, and not one
 *                   review link anywhere on the page
 *   early/normal  · a populated Charts card carrying a rank plate
 *   post-finalize · the finalized card + a Charts section whose empty ground
 *                   proves the NEW year's board starts empty (last year's
 *                   albums are gone from the home, not re-listed)
 *
 * Section heads carry a label and a title and nothing else (2026-09-07). The
 * Charts caption ("평론이 쌓일 때마다 바뀝니다 · 12월에 확정됩니다 · {날짜} 기준")
 * was cut with the same edit that cut /list/{year}/'s: a section that
 * explains what kind of section it is has stopped being a section and become
 * a note about the software. The heading names the surface; the cards under
 * it show what is on it.
 *
 * CHARTS IS A ONE-CARD-AT-A-TIME CAROUSEL (2026-09-10, decision-maker
 * request: "올해의 앨범은 원래처럼(기존 하나만 있었을때처럼 카드 하나가 폭 전체
 * 차지) 카드 한장만 뜨게 하고 최대 10개까지, 좌우 화살표버튼(동그랗고 반투명한
 * 버튼이 카드 양 옆에 있는 느낌), 버튼으로 한번에 한 카드씩 넘겨볼 수 있음,
 * 모바일도 동일" — the multi-card grid/scroller this section grew between
 * 2026-09-07 and 2026-09-09 read as too dense, and an intermediate pass on
 * this same date briefly cut it to a single, un-navigable card with no way
 * to reach anything but the leader. THAT PASS HAD THE REQUEST BACKWARDS: "카드
 * 하나가 폭 전체 차지" describes the CARD SHAPE the decision-maker wants
 * restored (`feature` — 440px cover + two-column body, unchanged since the W5
 * rebuild), not a ceiling of one card total. Reversed within the hour, and
 * recorded here rather than silently corrected, because the same misreading
 * is easy to make again from the sentence alone.
 *
 * "기존처럼" checks out against git history for the CARD, not the count:
 * `n-1`'s `display: block` plus ChartCard's own `feature` layout is the
 * shape a lone candidate has always taken here — it was simply gated behind
 * `charts.length === 1`. That gate is gone, but not in the direction the
 * intermediate pass took it: EVERY card in the row is `feature` now, always,
 * whatever `charts.length` is, so the one visible at any moment is the same
 * shape the site has always shown for a single candidate.
 *
 * ONE CARD VISIBLE, UP TO TEN REACHABLE. `charts` is capped at ten by
 * `deriveHomeSections` (via `deriveTop10`, lib/derive/lists.ts) and ordered
 * by the year's overall rank — see that function's own intro for why the
 * card is the year's #1 and not a bucket leader. All of them render into the
 * DOM in order; `.chart-cards` clips to exactly one card's width and scrolls
 * the rest offscreen, which is what makes "한 카드씩 넘겨볼 수 있음" true by
 * construction — stepping one card is stepping one flex-basis-100% child,
 * never a fraction of one.
 *
 * THE ARROWS ARE STRUCTURAL, NOT OVERLAID (the "카드 양 옆에 있는 느낌" this
 * asked for). ADR-0008 (ChartCard's own intro) forbids covering album art
 * except by the one recorded exception (the list thumbnail's score chip);
 * putting a translucent circle over the FEATURE cover — which runs flush to
 * the card's own left edge by design, and runs FULL WIDTH above the body on
 * the stacked mobile layout — would have been a second exception with
 * nothing but layout convenience to justify it, on every viewport, not only
 * the wide one. `.chart-carousel` sides with the rule instead: the two
 * buttons are siblings of the scroller, never `position: absolute` children
 * of it, so they occupy their own space rather than sitting on top of any
 * part of the card — see the style block for the two shapes that takes
 * (flanking from 641px up, stacked below the card under it) and why the
 * shape itself changes with viewport rather than staying one flex row down
 * to 360px.
 *
 * THE COST IS HONEST AND MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED, AT BOTH SHAPES. From 641px
 * up the visible card is `.chart-carousel`'s width minus two 44px circles
 * and two gaps; that arithmetic is FIXED-PIXEL, so it costs proportionally
 * more of a narrower row, and browser.hierarchy.spec.ts's own comment has
 * the measured floor: 63% of the section's width at 390px before the
 * stacked layout below 640px existed, which read as the cramped, crowded
 * card this rebuild was asked to fix in the first place — a real image, not
 * a hypothetical, driving the second CSS shape.
 *
 * NO ARROWS FOR A SINGLE CANDIDATE (R-4). `charts.length > 1` gates both
 * buttons — a control that can only ever point at the one card already on
 * screen is exactly the empty-shell shape R-4 forbids, and the real content
 * today (two reviews) makes this the common case rather than an edge case.
 *
 * SCRIPT IS AN ENHANCEMENT, NOT THE MECHANISM. `.chart-cards` is a native
 * horizontal scroll container with `scroll-snap-type: x mandatory` — a
 * reader can already move it one card at a time with a finger or a trackpad
 * with zero script running, which is what keeps browser.flows.spec.ts's
 * `javaScriptEnabled: false` context whole. The inline module
 * (enhance.js) only wires the two buttons to `scrollBy` one card and keeps
 * them `disabled` at either end; without it the buttons render (SSR sets the
 * first card's `prev` `disabled` up front) but do nothing when clicked —
 * which is an acceptable no-script gap because the row underneath them is
 * never the reader's only way to move it, only the buttons' own way.
 */
import ArticleCard from '../components/ArticleCard.astro';
import ChartCard from '../components/ChartCard.astro';
import FormatLabel from '../components/FormatLabel.astro';
import SectionEmpty from '../components/SectionEmpty.astro';
import SectionHead from '../components/SectionHead.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getSiteData } from '../lib/derive/site-data.ts';
import { SITE_TAGLINE } from '../lib/site.ts';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths.ts';

const { site, homeSections, homeVariant, data } = getSiteData();
const { charts, latestReviews, notes } = homeSections;

// Exactly one <h1> per document. Now that all three sections always render,
// Charts is always the first heading on the page — except in the `empty`
// variant, where the declaration line above it is the page's real title and
// takes the h1 instead.
const chartsLevel = homeVariant === 'empty' ? 'h2' : 'h1';

const previousYear = site.active_year - 1;
const previousSnapshot = data.snapshots.find((s) => s.data.year === previousYear);
---

<Base title={site.site_name} description={SITE_TAGLINE} path="/">
  <main class="home">
    {
      homeVariant === 'empty' && (
        <section class="empty-state">
          {/* The same promise /about/ opens with, in the words /about/ now
              uses. This line used to read "…“안 들으면 손해” 문턱을 통과한
              것입니다", which was About's first statement verbatim; that
              statement was retired in the 2026-09-07 rewrite, and leaving it
              here would have stranded the site's front page on a sentence
              the site no longer says anywhere else. No new copy was written
              — this is the confirmed About sentence. */}
          <h1 class="lede">여기 실린 앨범은 전부 추천작입니다.</h1>
          <p class="status">첫 평론을 준비하고 있습니다.</p>
        </section>
      )
    }

    {
      homeVariant === 'post-finalize' && previousSnapshot && (
        <section class="finalized-card">
          <FormatLabel text={`${previousYear} 올해의 앨범`} />
          <a class="finalized-link" href={withBase(`/list/${previousYear}/`)}>
            <span class="finalized-title">{previousYear} 올해의 앨범 — 확정</span>
            <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </section>
      )
    }

    <section class="board" aria-label="올해의 앨범">
      {/* `sheen` — the only heading on this page allowed the gradient
          display treatment: the annual list is what the scores produced,
          which is exactly what --accent is reserved for (tokens.css). The
          other two headings deliberately do NOT take it: "최신 리뷰" and
          "음악 이야기" are not lists the scores made, and painting them
          with the score's colour is the exact move that turns the doctrine
          back into decoration. */}
      <SectionHead label="Charts" title={`${site.active_year} 올해의 앨범`} level={chartsLevel} sheen />
      {
        charts.length > 0 ? (
          /* One-card-at-a-time carousel, up to ten reachable (2026-09-10 —
             see the intro). `.chart-carousel` is the three-way flex row
             (prev button · scroller · next button); the buttons are omitted
             entirely below `charts.length > 1` (R-4 — no control that can
             only ever point at what is already on screen). Every card is
             `feature`, always: the visible one is meant to read exactly like
             the single-candidate shape this section has always had. Only the
             first is `eager` — the rest scroll into view, not onto the
             critical rendering path. */
          <div class="chart-carousel">
            {
              charts.length > 1 && (
                /* `disabled`, not `aria-disabled`: a real `<button>` gets to
                   use the native state, which marks the end two ways at once
                   (dimmed AND pulled from the tab order / announced
                   "unavailable") without a second attribute to keep in sync
                   (WCAG 1.4.1 — never by colour alone). Set here rather than
                   left to the inline module because the carousel always
                   opens on card 0: the no-script page is already correct,
                   and enhance.js only re-derives the same value as the row
                   scrolls. */
                <button type="button" class="carousel-btn prev" data-scroll="-1" aria-label="이전 앨범" disabled>
                  <span aria-hidden="true">‹</span>
                </button>
              )
            }
            <ol class="chart-cards stagger">
              {charts.map((entry, i) => (
                <ChartCard entry={entry} feature eager={i === 0} />
              ))}
            </ol>
            {
              charts.length > 1 && (
                <button type="button" class="carousel-btn next" data-scroll="1" aria-label="다음 앨범">
                  <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
                </button>
              )
            }
          </div>
        ) : (
          <SectionEmpty />
        )
      }
      {/* Charts keeps its tail link even with no cards: /list/{year}/ always
          exists for active_year, whatever it currently shows (an empty top10
          section renders nothing there either, R-4 — see that page's own
          intro for the 2026-09-09 cut of its old always-there bucket board).
          That page IS the ranked list this section summarises, so an empty
          Charts section can still point at something real — which is what
          "let the structure speak" means here. The carousel above already
          reaches every candidate up to the ten-card cap; this link is what
          reaches the rest of the board past that cap, and it is still the
          only route with no script at all if a reader never discovers the
          carousel's own scroll affordance. */}
      <a class="section-more" href={withBase(`/list/${site.active_year}/`)}>올해의 앨범 전체 보기 →</a>
    </section>

    <section aria-label="최신 리뷰">
      <SectionHead label="Latest Reviews" title="최신 리뷰" />
      {
        latestReviews.length > 0 ? (
          <>
            <ul class="cards">
              {latestReviews.map((item, i) => (
                /* showScore — D2-R2: this is one of the two surfaces the
                   editor reversed D2 for. 음악 이야기 below deliberately
                   does not pass it (a story has no score to print). */
                /* showFormat={false} — the section head one line above already
                   says "Latest Reviews", and nothing but a review can appear
                   here, so a REVIEWS chip on every card is the same word
                   twice. The mixed archives keep theirs (see ArticleCard). */
                <ArticleCard item={item} variant="card" eager={i < 4} showScore showFormat={false} />
              ))}
            </ul>
            {/* No tail link when the section is empty: the archive it points
                at would be empty too, and sending a reader to a second empty
                page is worse than not offering the trip. Charts is the
                exception above, and for a reason. */}
            <a class="section-more" href={withBase('/archive/reviews/')}>평론 전체 보기 →</a>
          </>
        ) : (
          <SectionEmpty />
        )
      }
    </section>

    <section aria-label="음악 이야기">
      <SectionHead label="Notes" title="음악 이야기" />
      {
        notes.length > 0 ? (
          <>
            <ul class="cards">
              {notes.map((item) => (
                <ArticleCard item={item} variant="card" showFormat={false} />
              ))}
            </ul>
            <a class="section-more" href={withBase('/archive/stories/')}>이야기 전체 보기 →</a>
          </>
        ) : (
          <SectionEmpty />
        )
      }
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .home {
    width: min(var(--shell), 100% - 2 * var(--gutter));
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: var(--s-48) 0 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-48);
  }
  .empty-state .lede {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: var(--fs-27);
    line-height: 32px;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    font-weight: 800;
    margin: 0;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .empty-state .status {
    margin-top: var(--s-16);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--fs-14);
    line-height: 20px;
  }

  .finalized-card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-12);
  }
  .finalized-link {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    min-height: 44px;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: var(--fs-27);
    line-height: 32px;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    font-weight: 800;
  }
  .finalized-link:hover .finalized-title {
    color: var(--accent-2-ink);
  }
  .arrow {
    color: var(--accent-2-ink);
  }

  /* Section tail link — same object in all three sections. */
  .section-more {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    margin-top: var(--s-16);
    font-size: var(--fs-15);
    line-height: 20px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .section-more:hover {
    color: var(--accent-2-ink);
  }

  /* ── Lists: one rule set per section (layouts.md §2.4) ──────────────────
     `.chart-cards` and `.cards` share only list resets here (list-style,
     padding) — their sizing rules live apart because they are different
     objects now: `.chart-cards` is a horizontal scroll track inside
     `.chart-carousel` (below), `.cards` is a wrapping grid. Top margin moved
     OFF `.chart-cards` and onto `.chart-carousel` (2026-09-10) since the
     scroller is no longer the outermost element in the Charts section — see
     that rule for why. */
  .chart-cards,
  .cards {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .cards {
    margin-top: var(--s-16);
  }

  /* ── Charts: one-card carousel (2026-09-10 — see the intro) ─────────────
     Three flex children, always in this order: an optional `prev` button, the
     scroller, an optional `next` button. The buttons are OMITTED from the DOM
     (not just hidden) when `charts.length <= 1` — see the intro's R-4 note —
     so this rule never has to branch on whether they exist; `gap` simply
     collapses when a flex child is absent. */
  .chart-carousel {
    margin-top: var(--s-16);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--s-8);
  }

  /* NARROW SCREENS PUT THE BUTTONS UNDER THE CARD (2026-09-10, measured).

     Flanking circles cost a FIXED 2 x 44px plus two gaps whatever the
     viewport is, so the share they take grows as the screen shrinks. On the
     real build: 1200px gave the card 940px (78%), 390px gave it 246px
     (63%) -- and "one card taking the full width" is the whole brief for
     this section. A 63%-wide card with two circles crowding it is the
     cramped look this rebuild was asked to remove.

     `column` + `order` moves them below rather than shrinking them: a 44px
     touch target is the floor this site holds everywhere (masthead nav,
     archive chips), and trading it away on the one device that is actually
     touched would be the wrong side to give. Below the card they are a
     centred pair, still 44px, still one card per press.

     Overlaying them on the card edges was the other option and is refused:
     the left edge of this card IS the cover, and this site does not lay
     anything over album art (ADR-0008 -- the list thumbnail score chip is
     the single documented exception, and its reasoning is written out in
     ArticleCard). */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .chart-carousel {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      justify-items: center;
      gap: var(--s-12) var(--s-8);
    }
    .chart-carousel .chart-cards {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
      grid-row: 1;
      width: 100%;
    }
    .carousel-btn.prev {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 2;
      justify-self: end;
    }
    .carousel-btn.next {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 2;
      justify-self: start;
    }
  }
  /* The scroller. `flex: 1` lets it take whatever width the two buttons
     leave; `min-width: 0` is required on a flex item before that — without
     it a flex item's default `min-width: auto` refuses to shrink below its
     CONTENT's own minimum, which for a horizontally-scrolling row is its
     full unscrolled length, and the whole carousel would refuse to shrink
     below ten cards wide. `scroll-snap-type: x mandatory` plus each card's
     `scroll-snap-align: start` below is what makes "step one card" the
     resting state after either a script-driven `scrollBy` or a bare-finger
     swipe — the same mechanism serves both (see the intro's script-is-an-
     enhancement paragraph). `scrollbar-width: thin`, not `none`: a visible
     scrollbar is the one hint a mouse/trackpad reader gets that the row
     holds more than what is on screen when JavaScript never runs to draw the
     arrows' disabled state for them. `overscroll-behavior-x: contain` keeps
     a swipe that reaches either end from bubbling into the page's own
     scroll — the contained-scroll property browser.responsive.spec.ts
     measures is the DOCUMENT'S width, and this is what keeps this row from
     ever being counted against it. */
  .chart-cards {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    overflow-x: auto;
    scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
    overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
    scrollbar-width: thin;
    /* Room for the focus outline on a snapped card and for the scrollbar
       thumb, so neither is clipped by the container's own overflow box. */
    padding-bottom: var(--s-8);
  }
  /* `:global` reaches into ChartCard's own scoped output — Astro scopes a
     component's root element with an attribute selector, and a plain
     `.chart-cards > .chart-card` written here would carry THIS file's scope
     attribute instead and never match. `flex: 0 0 100%` is the one rule that
     makes "one card visible, the rest offscreen" true regardless of how many
     `charts` holds: every card is exactly the scroller's own width, never a
     fraction sized by card count. */
  .chart-cards > :global(.chart-card) {
    flex: 0 0 100%;
    min-width: 0;
    scroll-snap-align: start;
  }
  /* Circular, translucent — the decision-maker's own words ("동그랗고
     반투명한 버튼"), and the FIRST use of `--r-full` (tokens.css) since square
     is this system's default everywhere else. `color-mix` rather than a
     literal `rgba()` keeps the fill a function of the existing `--surface-3`
     token instead of a new hardcoded colour — the only new value this rule
     introduces is the mix ratio, not a colour. 44px is the site's touch
     target floor (Masthead, ArticleCard's row date, everywhere else on the
     page already holds it); `appearance: none` strips the UA's own button
     chrome so the circle is the whole visible shape, and `padding: 0` keeps
     the glyph centred inside it rather than pushed by a default inset. */
  .carousel-btn {
    flex: none;
    appearance: none;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 44px;
    height: 44px;
    padding: 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    border-radius: var(--r-full);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--surface-3) 65%, transparent);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: var(--fs-21);
    line-height: 1;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: background var(--t-micro), border-color var(--t-micro), color var(--t-micro), opacity var(--t-micro);
  }
  .carousel-btn:hover:not(:disabled) {
    background: var(--surface-3);
    color: var(--accent-2-ink);
  }
  /* Native `disabled`, marked two ways at once rather than by colour alone
     (WCAG 1.4.1) — see the markup comment for why `disabled` and not
     `aria-disabled` here. The link-based pager this replaced (git history)
     had to fall back to `aria-disabled` because an anchor has no native
     disabled state; a `<button>` does, so this carousel does not need the
     older pattern's second attribute. */
  .carousel-btn:disabled {
    opacity: 0.4;
    cursor: default;
  }

  /* ── Browsing grids: ONE grid, four columns, stepped at three widths ──
     (2026-09-07. Replaced `auto-fill`, then the per-count `n-*` variants.)

        viewport      columns   browsing card
        ≥ 1160px         4      241 → 320px
        840 – 1159       3      244 → 320px
        641 – 839        2      292 → 320px
        ≤ 640            1      full width

     The chart card's own width is no longer in this table (2026-09-10): it
     is not a grid track, it is `.chart-carousel`'s flex width minus its two
     buttons — see that rule and browser.hierarchy.spec.ts, which measures
     the two against each other directly rather than relying on a table like
     this one to stay in sync with both.

     THE CARD COUNT IS NOT IN THAT TABLE, and that is the whole change: a
     section holding one card renders it in the first cell at the same width
     a section holding four would. The three empty cells are exactly that —
     empty. Nothing is drawn for a grid cell with no item in it, and every
     border, surface and radius on this page belongs to a CARD rather than to
     the grid, so a short section reads as a short row and never as a broken
     one.

     The breakpoints are where a track would otherwise fall under 240px, the
     narrowest a compact card stays readable at, computed against the gutter
     curve in tokens.css: 4 x 240 + 3 x 16 needs 1008px of content, which
     arrives at a 1160px viewport; 3-up needs 752 (840px); 2-up needs 496
     (641px, reused from the existing mobile breakpoint rather than invented
     at 540 so that every grid on this page collapses at the same width).

     `minmax(0, 320px)`, NOT `1fr`, IS THE HIERARCHY RULE IN CSS — FROM 641px
     UP. A fixed max makes the track min(320, available/n): it fills the row
     when the row is narrow and STOPS at 320 when the row is wide, so a
     browsing card can never reach the visible chart card's own width at any
     multi-column width — including the ≥1680px case where the shell caps the
     frame at 1440 and four `1fr` tracks would resolve to 348px and invert the
     hierarchy. The `0` minimum is what keeps a card's own content (the nowrap
     subtitle) from blowing the track out at the narrow end.

     BELOW 641px THE GRID IS TWO COLUMNS, NOT ONE (2026-09-08). It was a
     single `1fr` track — see the superseded paragraph below for why that was
     the right call in 2026-09-07 — until the brief changed: the reader wants
     four cards readable without scrolling past them one at a time ("최신리뷰
     카드 4개 보였으면좋겠어 1 2 / 아래로 3 4"). Two 152px-ish tracks is
     narrower than the row variant's dense archive listing but wider than it
     sounds — see ArticleCard's card-variant type scale, already the smallest
     step on the site, chosen for exactly this box. The hierarchy invariant
     (chart card ≥ browsing card) is UNCHANGED here: `.chart-carousel` is a
     flex row at every width, mobile included — see that rule — so it was
     never going to shrink below its own arithmetic (frame minus two fixed
     44px buttons) the way a grid track can shrink with a row it shares with
     other tracks; two half-width browsing tracks only widen the margin the
     invariant already held at 1fr. browser.hierarchy.spec.ts's 361–640px
     case is rewritten to match — see that spec for the property it now
     checks instead of "one card fills the row".

     THE SUPERSEDED PARAGRAPH, kept because the reasoning inside it (why a
     capped track breaks a genuinely single-column row) still applies the
     moment this becomes one column again at any future width: the cap was on
     the base rule too, which made it bite the one range where it has nothing
     to hold back: between 361 and 640px there was one track, so
     min(320, available) simply clipped the card and left the rest of the row
     empty. Measured at 600px before that fix — grid 20→580, card 20→340,
     while the chart card, the headings and the footer all ran to 580.

     TWO TRACKS, NOT `minmax(0, 320px)` — the cap exists to stop a track from
     out-growing the chart card at wide multi-column widths (see the ladder
     above); at 152px a track is nowhere near that ceiling, and `1fr` reaches
     it with one rule instead of a redundant `min()`. Gap and gutter are both
     fixed pixel values below 641px (`--gutter` clamps to its 20px floor for
     every viewport under ~673px — see tokens.css), so ArticleCard's `sizes`
     hint below can compute the exact rendered width instead of guessing. */
  .cards {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-16);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
  }
  @media (min-width: 641px) {
    .cards {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 320px));
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 840px) {
    .cards {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 320px));
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 1160px) {
    .cards {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 320px));
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .home {
      padding-top: var(--s-32);
    }
  }

  /* Board load orchestration — the site's ONE entrance animation: opacity +
     8px rise, 240ms, no per-child delay. Every card in `charts` carries this
     rule (all ten, when the board holds that many), but only the first is
     ever on screen at load — the rest sit clipped inside the scroller's
     `overflow-x`, so animating them in lockstep rather than staggering
     costs nothing visually; a reader only ever sees ONE entrance regardless
     of how many cards are behind it. This used to stagger up to five entries
     at 60ms apart (`--t-board-stagger`, tokens.css) back when the section
     rendered a wrapping grid with several cards visible at once; that reason
     stopped applying the day Charts became single-card, and the per-child
     delay rules were removed with it. `--t-board-stagger` itself was left in
     tokens.css rather than deleted here — a token is the design system's
     call, not this page's, and a single unused custom property costs
     nothing to leave for that decision. */
  .stagger > :global(*) {
    animation: board-in var(--t-board-row) var(--t-board-ease) both;
  }
  @keyframes board-in {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(8px);
    }
    to {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: translateY(0);
    }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .stagger > :global(*) {
      animation: none;
    }
  }
</style>
